// A declaration with an explicit type has to agree with its initializer.
//
// `var` infers the type from the initializer, so there is nothing to check. A
// written-out type is a claim about what the variable holds, and a claim that is
// not checked is worse than no claim: `int x = "hello";` compiled, and reading
// `x` gave back the pointer's own bytes as an integer.
//
// The one conversion Z makes is an int widening to a float, which cannot lose a
// value, and a concrete value going to an interface, which boxes it. Everything
// else has to match exactly, exactly as an assignment does.
int x = "hello";
